Hi all,

Tomorrow I will give a talk on using lapply to automate, using apply to do things that are easy in Excel but can seem hard in R, and writing your own functions. The idea is that a few key functions and idioms unlock a lot of power, but many researchers never come across them and so struggle with data manipulation and copy-and-paste code to do repetitive computations. It should be pretty beginner-friendly, and I am happy for it to be interactive, so if you have been struggling with something like this or have questions that fit the topic, bring them. As always, we will livecast (link <https://plus.google.com/events/cchhif4q8q00k7u495me3i4v6q0>), and make the video available afterward. Here is the blurb:
>
> ​Access the power of R through the apply-family of functions and by writing your own functions. You’ll save time, headaches, and mistakes. This is an “advanced-beginner” talk intended to get people started or more comfortable with automation. If you find yourself copy-and-pasting R code, this is for you! If you have multiple data files to process, this is for you! If you want to become an R wizard but aren’t sure what the next steps are, this is for you!

The talk is Monday at 1:10 in Wickson 1017. ​After the talk, we’ll have the usual work session until 3:00.

See you tomorrow,

Michael Levy

PhD Candidate
Department of Environmental Science and Policy
University of California, Davis
